From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Sat Feb  6 14:30:22 2016
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Sat, 06 Feb 2016 14:30:22 +0000
Subject: Re: X2 statistics in mirt
Message-ID: <9_acJKv1acg.Z5l78833BwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <9_acJKv1acg.xP2k1hbyBwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <9_acJKv1acg.xP2k1hbyBwAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/9_acJKv1acg/m/Z5l78833BwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This can happen with the MHRM algorithm because it is stochastic, so parameters are only approximations of the true ML estimates. However, note that these models are not nested, so you can't really use a X2 test anyway. Use the information criteria like AIC and BIC to inform your modeling decision (in this case the UIRT model fits about as well as the MIRT, so nothing is really gained). You could also compare goodness of fit with M2() to see if there is any modeling improvements, but I doubt it given how close these models match the data.

Phil

